-- The cable step could never be finished, so the chain dead-ended there.
--
-- Reported directly: "I want to go to system, but I don't understand from the
-- Cable AWG Calculator how to complete. What selection can we make to complete
-- everything here?" There was no answer, because there was no such selection.
--
-- Keep in sync with supabase/seed.sql (CI enforces this).

update public.roadmap_items
  set dev_percent_complete = 60,
      description = 'Initial version shipped 2026-09-01 as /calculators/system, step 7. The item asked for one flow with assumptions carried visibly, uncertainty shown where it enters, and a stated confidence band -- and named the failure it exists to prevent: "four tools that each look authoritative and quietly disagree is the worst configuration available." That is not hypothetical. Panel sizing rounds UP to a whole count from an energy target; array wiring must factor that count into whole strings inside a voltage window and a current limit. For the SG550WM against an SPH10048P at a -25.9 degC design low, EVERY ODD COUNT fails -- 9, 11, 13 and 15 have no safe arrangement while 10, 12, 14 and 16 do. The panel page says buy 11, the array page says nothing fits, and neither is wrong on its own terms. src/lib/system-design.ts does three things no single step structurally can: DISAGREEMENTS (the panel-count mismatch, a battery and inverter at different voltages, an array past the PV input, string fusing needed or unknown, panels sized but never wired), each with what each side says, why neither is wrong alone, what to do, and links back to both steps; ASSUMPTIONS WITH PROVENANCE -- every figure that shapes the result, which step set it, whether it is still the shipped default, what it changes downstream and how much it moves the answer, ranked by leverage rather than by step; and a CONFIDENCE BAND that is deliberately a word rather than a percentage, because a numeric interval would need error distributions this app does not have. A value equal to the shipped default reads as "still at the default", never "you did not choose this". 2026-09-02, from direct feedback: THE CABLE STEP COULD NEVER BE COMPLETED. It was the only step that publishes nothing, because it is a tool run repeatedly -- once per cable run -- rather than a step with one answer, so every other step could be detected done and this one never could. The chain dead-ended there. It now publishes a ProtectionSummary keyed by run: the user picks a gauge from the set that passed (the conductor output still refuses to name one -- that is the protection register, and recording which one was CHOSEN is the honest way to remember a decision the calculator declined to make), saves it, and the page shows "N of M runs sized" with what remains. Completion means every applicable run has a gauge, which is why the combiner run is excluded when there is one string per tracker. The system page gained a cable schedule -- run, current, length, gauge, drop and the protecting device -- which is the first real step toward the bill of materials. A drift affordance was added at the same time: redoing an upstream step leaves the cable fields holding yesterday figures, so when they disagree with the chain the page offers the current ones rather than silently overwriting, matching the suggestedDuty pattern. Also fixed: confidence() no longer excludes protection as permanently undetectable. NOT DONE: printable bill of materials and cost estimate.'
  where title = 'System designer: one integrated sizing chain';
